abstract: >-
  Lean 4.35.0 (Mathlib v4.35.0) proofs and exact-arithmetic verifiers for three
  Pauli contextuality tracks: (1) a formalization of Jonah's spectral and Kramers
  floors (prior work) for arbitrary supports, an infinite family of non-closed
  pure-CCZ Pauli sets |Psi_{3m}> attaining the floor (contextual for m >= 2),
  exact binary completion optima 368 and 344 for one four-qubit C3 sign pair,
  lower bounds d(W(7,2)) >= 1455 and d(Q+(9,2)) >= 6444 (Jonah earlier claimed
  the exact values 1575 and 6975), two-sided asymptotic half-violation limits
  (1/2 - eps < d/L <= 1/2 for W, Q-, Q+), and, for Jonah's Conjecture E,
  strict-law failure for one explicit representative formally certified inside
  each of the nine geometric vertex-case predicates for all N >= 4 (whole-case
  transfer via Jonah's Theorem B4, outside Lean); (2) exact contextuality
  degrees (7^k - 5^k)/2 and (6^k - 4^k)/2 for k disjoint Peres-Mermin squares
  and Mermin pentagrams, heterogeneous endpoint reduction, mixed (s,t) family
  degrees (7^s 6^t - 5^s 4^t)/2, general finite contextual seed asymptotics
  (eps_k -> 1), arbitrary-k 2k- and 3k-qubit complex Pauli tensor-product
  realizations, k-independent readout-noise thresholds, and kernel-checked
  HS(L(K_{3,3})) / HS(Petersen) support identifications for all k, answering
  the stacking-limit question of Muller and Saniga (arXiv:2609.19862v1, Sec. 6)
  for these two families; and (3) formalized elementary bounds for sequential contextuality
  evidence and a setting-bias example on the Peres-Mermin square. Both external
  sources are unreviewed preprints. Not externally peer reviewed; AI-assisted
  under human direction.
